\section{Introduction}

Recurrent neural networks (RNNs) are highly effective tools for addressing sequential tasks, such as next character or word prediction, as well as sequence generation. Despite their advantages, the backpropagation-through-time (BPTT) algorithm used to train RNNs is notoriously difficult to implement and often converges to suboptimal local minima. Consequently, there is a pressing need to explore alternative training methodologies. This study proposes a solution by merging evolutionary strategies with unsupervised objectives. Specifically, we integrate the Covariance Matrix Adaptation Evolution Strategy (CMA-ES) \cite{N{\'a}s{\'a}nelli2021} with either an autoregressive objective or a reconstruction objective. Our results indicate that this approach markedly improves the quality of unsupervised samples produced by RNNs. Additionally, even in scenarios where supervised objectives exist, the inclusion of these unsupervised components leads to notable improvements. We validate these claims through experiments on next character prediction, next word prediction, and sequence generation using an attention-based method. Across all tasks, the implementation of evolutionary strategies yielded statistically significant gains. The accompanying source code can be found at \url{https://github.com/evolutionary-systems/evolutionary-recurrent-networks}.

